From 7ea9a61d3f97f688df89e92ed315c96d5524a07a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:39:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Desktop: ride session writes (archive/pin/unread)
 across a hub reconnect

Archive (and every fenced session write) now waits out a remote backend
reconnect and replays the same intent, instead of failing on the first
socket hang up / down-gate fail-fast and making the user click again.

Evidence (laptop-1 desktop.log, 2026-11-12 21:10 -> 2026-11-13 00:13 PST,
bundle ea936d5d20): nine hub PATCH /api/sessions/<id> failures on
conn=hermes-hub - "socket hang up", the remote-down gate's "Remote backend
... is down ...; reconnect in progress. Failing fast instead of waiting for
a timeout.", "Passive read: no warm backend", and dial timeouts - each in a
[session-freshness] gateway-unavailable window that ended seconds later.

Five whys (fail-fast path):
1. Why did the archive fail? The PATCH hit a hub tunnel reset (socket hang
   up), and the next tries were refused at once by the remote-down gate.
2. Why refused at once? The gate (electron/remote-down-gate.ts) fails every
   request fast while the hub is down, so UI polls don't stack 4-30 s
   timeouts; it lets one trial through per backoff window.
3. Why did that end the user's archive? mutateSessionHttp sent the fenced
   write exactly once; any rejection went to commitArchive, which restored
   the row and showed a Retry toast.
4. Why was there no automatic replay? The write was treated like a read:
   nothing distinguished "the route to the owner is down" from a verdict,
   although the mutation client already retains the request_id/expected
   revision for a non-definitive failure, so a replay is idempotent.
5. Why was that not used? Retry was only wired to the explicit toast button
   (5da2d6c856); no layer below it waited for the reconnect.

Fix: withSessionWriteReconnect (src/api/session-write-reconnect.ts) wraps
mutateSessionHttp. A transport failure (hang up, ECONN*, down-gate refusal,
dial timeout, passive no-warm-backend) is retried with 1/2/4/8/10 s backoff,
never earlier than the gate's own "next try in Ns", for up to 60 s, replaying
the retained identity (same request_id, same expected revision), so an owner
that already committed answers from its receipt. The archive row stays
optimistically hidden meanwhile. Backend answers are never retried here:
definitive refusals (conflict, not found, client_upgrade_required, ...) and
503 owner_unavailable (already ridden out server-side) still reach the user.
Failure stays visible: past the window the existing restore + "Archive
failed" toast with Retry runs, and every replay, landing and give-up writes
a [session-write] line to desktop.log.

The 409 client_upgrade_required failures (conn=primary, which on the MBP is
the hub: "conn:hermes-hub::default via=primary") were not archives and not
app/backend drift: bundle ea936d5d20 is an ancestor of core 2cc7101849 (2
commits apart). They are the hermes-mods inbox plugin's markRead, a raw
PATCH {unread:false} with no request_id/expected_revision, fired on focus
(24 of them on Nov 12 repeat every ~30 s per focused chat); the backend
correctly names an identity-less write client_upgrade_required. The plugin
swallows the error, so the user saw nothing. Fixed separately in hermes-mods
(follow-up card); this commit pins that such a refusal is never replayed.

Tests: session-write-reconnect.test.ts (each logged hub failure replays;
each backend answer does not; gate hint honoured; budget gives the error
back) and sessions.test.ts (a hub archive that hits hang-up then fail-fast
lands on the third PATCH with one request_id; client_upgrade_required is
sent once). RED on the old sessions.ts: the reconnect test fails.
